---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/for/beauty';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for/beauty');
const desc = description(site, 'for/beauty');
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/for" class="text-sm font-bold text-[#9a5b16] hover:text-[#24170f]">← All store types</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Beauty and skincare</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Best Shopify email apps for beauty brands</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
        For most beauty stores on Shopify, Klaviyo is the best first pick. It reads order and product data at the item level, so a replenishment timer can key off the 30 ml serum a customer actually bought rather than a fixed calendar date. Its Email plan is listed at $20 a month for 500 profiles, and its free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month. Stores with a small catalog, a short list and little beyond a welcome series can often start for less with Omnisend or Shopify Email.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-[#5d3a1d]">
        The prices on this page are the figures printed on each vendor's public pricing page, read on 7 October 2026. Where a vendor does not show a price for the list size you need, the table says "pricing on request", and you should ask for a written quote before you budget.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Full app rankings</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment guide</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <TldrBox
      title="Quick picks for beauty stores on Shopify"
      picks={[
        { label: 'Best overall', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Product-level Shopify data drives replenishment and routine timing. The Email plan is listed at $20 a month for 500 profiles.' },
        { label: 'Best low-cost start', tool: 'Shopify Email', reason: 'The first 10,000 emails each month are free, then $1 per 1,000 emails, which suits a store still learning what its customers respond to.' },
        { label: 'Best for a small catalog with SMS', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'A free plan for 250 contacts, a Standard plan listed at $11.20 a month for 500 contacts and 6,000 emails, and email, SMS and push in one builder.' },
        { label: 'Best for pop-up capture', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Pop-ups, forms and email on one account. Privy Email is listed at $30 a month with unlimited emails, billed on mailable contacts.' },
        { label: 'Best for tag-based paths', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Visual workflows built on tags such as routine step or skin concern, listed from $39 a month for 1 to 2,500 people.' },
      ]}
    />
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">What beauty email has to do</h2>
      <p>
        Beauty email has four jobs that most other retail verticals do not share. The first is replenishment. A customer who bought a 30 ml serum and uses it twice a day will finish it sooner than someone who bought a 50 ml bottle and uses it on alternate nights. A reminder sent on a fixed 30-day schedule will therefore arrive too early for one customer and too late for another, and both will notice. The timing has to follow the product and the customer's usage. That means the email app needs to know what was bought, in what size, and when.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second job is education. Routines, layering order, patch-test advice and ingredient explanations are part of what a beauty customer pays for. They are also the content most likely to be ignored when it reaches the wrong person. A new buyer who has just received a cleanser needs to know how to use it. A loyal buyer on the third product in a routine does not. An email app that cannot segment by purchase history will send both the same beginner lesson, and the loyal buyer will eventually unsubscribe.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third job is subscription recovery. Many beauty brands sell through subscriptions run by apps such as Recharge, Skio or Bold. Those apps handle billing. The email app has to read subscription state and stay quiet when a charge is about to process or a skip is already in progress. A replenishment reminder that lands on the same day as a charge is one of the quickest ways to lose a subscriber's trust, and it happens often when the billing app and the email app do not share data.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fourth job is compliance and consent. Cosmetic labeling and claims are regulated. In the United States the FDA oversees cosmetic labeling, and in the European Union the Cosmetics Regulation governs what can be said about a product. Email is a fast way to put a claim in front of a large audience, so urgency copy and ingredient promises should pass a compliance reviewer before they go out. SMS adds its own consent rules. In the US, marketing texts need prior express consent under the Telephone Consumer Protection Act, and in the EU, consent records must meet GDPR standards. Each app on this page can store consent in some form, but none of them removes the duty to keep records and use them correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep these four jobs in mind as you read the rest of the page. The right app for a beauty store is the one that handles all four at your list size and send volume without a large monthly bill for features you will not use.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
      <p>
        The table below sets each app against the questions a beauty merchant usually asks first: what it is best at for this vertical, what it costs to start, whether a free option exists, and the main limitation to plan around. Prices are the starting figures on each vendor's pricing page. Read the plan details before you commit, because the entry price usually applies to a small list.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="ledger-card mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[760px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/15 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-[#9a5b16]">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">App</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Best fit for beauty</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Entry price (plan, billing)</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Free option</th>
            <th class="py-3">Main limitation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[#4a2d17]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Replenishment and routine flows keyed to product and subscription status</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Email plan, 500 profiles, $20 a month</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month</td>
            <td class="py-3">Cost rises with every profile, including people who never buy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Smaller catalogs that want email, SMS and push in one builder</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Standard, 500 contacts and 6,000 emails, $11.20 a month</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">250 contacts, 500 emails and 500 web push notifications a month</td>
            <td class="py-3">The $11.20 figure is shown as a first-three-month offer rate, and the pricing page does not show a price above 2,500 contacts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/shopify-email">Shopify Email</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stores that want low-cost sending from the Shopify admin</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">First 10,000 emails each month free, then $1 per 1,000 emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Yes, the first 10,000 emails each month</td>
            <td class="py-3">Billing follows send volume, so frequent reminder schedules cost more as the store grows</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/mailchimp">Mailchimp</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Newsletters and a basic welcome series on a small list</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Standard, 500-contact tier, $20 a month, up to 6,000 emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Up to 250 contacts and 500 sends a month, capped at 250 a day</td>
            <td class="py-3">The free plan's daily cap blocks most sale-day sends</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pop-up and quiz capture feeding an email program on one account</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Privy Email, $30 a month, unlimited emails, billed on mailable contacts</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">No free plan</td>
            <td class="py-3">No free plan, and the bill depends on how many contacts are mailable</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]"><a class="link" href="/apps/drip">Drip</a></td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stores that want explicit control over tag-based education paths</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">1 to 2,500 people, $39 a month, unlimited email sends</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">No ongoing free plan; 14-day trial, no credit card required</td>
            <td class="py-3">Pricing moves through a long tier table by people count, so the right plan depends on exact list size</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">Seguno</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Listed in this vertical set; see the Seguno section below</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pricing on request</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">None listed</td>
            <td class="py-3">No published price to set against the others, so a written quote is needed</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">What each app costs at realistic list sizes</h2>
      <p>
        A starting price says little about the bill a beauty store will receive in its second year. The table below shows what each app charges at five list sizes, using the plan names and send allowances printed on each pricing page. Where a vendor does not show a price for that size, the cell says "pricing on request" instead of an estimate. Figures are monthly and do not include SMS, which is priced separately on every app.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="ledger-card mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[860px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/15 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-[#9a5b16]">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Contacts</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Klaviyo</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Omnisend</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Mailchimp</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Drip</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Shopify Email</th>
            <th class="py-3">Privy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[#4a2d17]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">250</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Free plan: 250 active profiles, 500 email sends a month</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Free plan: 250 contacts, 500 emails a month</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Free plan: up to 250 contacts, 500 sends a month</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$39 a month, 1 to 2,500 people tier</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$0 while sends stay under 10,000 a month</td>
            <td class="py-3">$30 a month, Privy Email, mailable contacts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">500</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$20 a month, Email plan at 500 profiles</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$11.20 a month, Standard, 6,000 emails (first-three-month offer rate)</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$20 a month, Standard, up to 6,000 emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$39 a month, 1 to 2,500 people tier</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$0 while sends stay under 10,000 a month</td>
            <td class="py-3">$30 a month, Privy Email, mailable contacts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">2,500</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$60 a month, Email plan at 2,500 profiles</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$41.30 a month, Pro, 2,500 contacts with unlimited emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pricing on request for 1,501 to 2,500 contacts; the next tier shown starts at 2,501</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$39 a month, 1 to 2,500 people tier</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$0 while sends stay under 10,000 a month</td>
            <td class="py-3">$30 a month, Privy Email, mailable contacts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">5,000</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$100 a month, Email plan at 5,000 profiles</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pricing on request; no tier at this size is shown</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$100 a month, 2,501 to 5,000 contacts, up to 60,000 emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$89 a month, 4,501 to 5,000 people</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Depends on sends; see the worked example below</td>
            <td class="py-3">$30 a month listed; confirm against your mailable count</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">10,000</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$150 a month, Email plan at 10,000 profiles</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pricing on request</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$135 a month, 5,001 to 10,000 contacts, up to 120,000 emails</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$154 a month, 9,001 to 10,000 people</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Depends on sends; the first 10,000 emails each month are free</td>
            <td class="py-3">$30 a month listed; confirm against your mailable count</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl mt-8">
      <p>
        Two readings of this table matter most for beauty. The first is that Klaviyo and Drip charge for people on the list, while Shopify Email charges for sends. A store that imports a large sampling or influencer list and mails it rarely may pay less on Shopify Email than on a profile-based plan. A store that mails a focused replenishment list every week may find the opposite.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is the gap between the 2,500 and 5,000 rows. Klaviyo rises from $60 to $100 a month on its Email plan, and Mailchimp moves from the $20 Standard tier to $100 at 2,501 to 5,000 contacts. Those jumps are where budgets usually go wrong, because beauty lists tend to grow faster than repeat purchases in the first year. The worked example further down puts numbers on that growth.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container space-y-12 pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Klaviyo: the replenishment and segmentation default</h2>
      <p>
        Klaviyo is the app to start with for a beauty store that expects replenishment and routine education to be core revenue programs. Its strength is the link between Shopify order data and segmentation. A flow can branch on what a customer bought, how large the product was, whether the customer is in an active subscription, and whether they opened the last two education emails. Those branches are the building blocks of a serum replenishment flow that runs on a product-size timer rather than a calendar date.
      </p>
      <p>
        For beauty, the practical benefit is precision. A store selling a 15 ml eye cream, a 30 ml serum and a 50 ml cleanser can give each product its own reorder timer and its own education sequence. It can also suppress a flow as soon as an order for the same item arrives. The app supports separate segments for one-time buyers, repeat buyers and subscribers, which lets a brand keep loyal customers out of beginner routines. A subscriber who has just reordered should not receive a "your first serum is running low" message, and segment logic is what stops that.
      </p>
      <p>
        The limits are about cost and discipline. Pricing is per profile, so every person who joins from a sampling form or a quiz and never buys still counts toward the plan. The free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month, enough to build and test flows but not enough for a live store with a real list. The Email plan is listed at $20 a month for 500 profiles, $60 for 2,500 profiles and $150 for 10,000 profiles. SMS is priced separately, and the Email plus SMS tiers do not show a price on the pricing page, so SMS costs need a quote.
      </p>
      <p>
        Klaviyo fits a beauty brand with an owner or agency responsible for retention, a Shopify catalog large enough to justify product-level flows, and a willingness to prune inactive profiles. It is a weaker fit for a brand with one hero product and a list of a few hundred people, where similar flows can be built in a cheaper tool.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">klaviyo.com/pricing</a>. For the replenishment setup itself, see the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment guide</a>. For a side-by-side with Omnisend, see the <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend comparison</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Omnisend: a smaller catalog that wants SMS and push</h2>
      <p>
        Omnisend is the strongest option on this page for a beauty store with a smaller catalog that wants email, SMS and web push from one builder. The free plan covers 250 contacts, 500 emails and 500 web push notifications a month, so a new store can set up a welcome series, a cart sequence and a post-purchase education flow before paying anything. The Standard plan is listed at $11.20 a month for 500 contacts and 6,000 emails. That figure is shown on the pricing page as a first-three-month offer rate, so check what the plan costs once the offer ends. The Pro plan is listed at $41.30 a month for 2,500 contacts, with unlimited monthly emails and SMS starting at $0.007 per message.
      </p>
      <p>
        For beauty, the case for Omnisend is a single place to run a replenishment reminder by email and a short SMS nudge to the same customer, with one set of consent and suppression rules. That matters because a second reminder on another channel in the same week is where many beauty stores lose subscribers. Keeping both channels in one tool makes the suppression logic easier to check.
      </p>
      <p>
        The limits are in the price table. The pricing page does not show a tier above 2,500 contacts, so a store past that point needs a quote before it commits. Segmentation for large shade matrices and detailed subscription branching is also a question to test, not assume. Build the actual shade and subscription flows during the trial and see whether the logic you need can be expressed without workarounds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Omnisend suits a beauty brand in its first or second year, with one to three hero products, a list still under 2,500 contacts and an interest in SMS for replenishment. It is a poorer fit for a brand that expects to pass 10,000 contacts within six months and needs predictable pricing at that size.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://www.omnisend.com/pricing/">omnisend.com/pricing</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Shopify Email: low-cost sending inside the Shopify admin</h2>
      <p>
        Shopify Email is the lowest-cost route to a live beauty email program for a store already run from the Shopify admin. The price structure is simple: the first 10,000 emails each month are free, and each 1,000 emails after that cost $1. A store that sends 30,000 emails in a month pays for 20,000 of them, which is 20 blocks of 1,000 at $1 each, or $20 for the month. For a store still testing whether email drives repeat orders, the free allowance is the main attraction.
      </p>
      <p>
        The value for beauty is in the early phase. A store can send a welcome message, a post-purchase thank-you with a patch-test reminder and a first replenishment email to a few thousand customers without a monthly subscription. Because billing is per email rather than per profile, a sampling list that never buys does not raise the bill until it is mailed. That changes the economics for brands that bring in large lists through influencer codes but send to them rarely.
      </p>
      <p>
        The limit is that the price follows send volume. A beauty brand that adds a reorder reminder, a routine education series, a skip recovery sequence and a promotional calendar will send far more email than a brand with one newsletter, and the bill will follow. A store that wants per-customer flow logic, shade-level branching or detailed subscription suppression should test that depth in a trial and compare it with Klaviyo or Drip, because those details sit outside the pricing page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shopify Email suits a beauty store with mostly one-off buying, a small set of hero products and a plan to move to a dedicated platform once replenishment becomes a larger share of revenue.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-email/email-campaigns/pricing">Shopify's Shopify Email pricing help page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Mailchimp: simple sends for a small beauty list</h2>
      <p>
        Mailchimp is a sensible choice for a beauty brand whose email needs are mostly newsletters and a basic welcome series, with little replenishment logic. The free plan covers up to 250 contacts and 500 sends a month, with a limit of 250 sends a day. The Standard plan starts at $20 a month for the 500-contact tier, which includes up to 6,000 emails a month. At 2,501 to 5,000 contacts the listed price is $100 a month for up to 60,000 emails, and at 5,001 to 10,000 contacts it is $135 a month for up to 120,000 emails.
      </p>
      <p>
        For beauty, Mailchimp is most useful for a launch newsletter, a restock announcement or a seasonal shade guide sent to a list that does not need deep branching. A store with a loyal but modest customer base can run a clean newsletter here with little setup.
      </p>
      <p>
        The daily cap on the free plan is the practical problem. A product launch or a sale can need several thousand sends in one day, and the free plan's 250-per-day limit will stop that. Stores that expect launch days should price the Standard plan before the launch, not during it. The replenishment and routine education tools are narrower than those of Klaviyo or Drip, so a brand that depends on product-timed reorder flows should check the workflow builder against its plan before choosing Mailchimp.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://mailchimp.com/pricing/">mailchimp.com/pricing</a>. The pricing page shows a 14-day Standard trial, which is useful for testing a launch-day send before the daily cap becomes a problem.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Privy: pop-up capture with email on the same account</h2>
      <p>
        Privy is built around capture. Its pop-ups, sign-up forms and exit intents are the reason many beauty stores look at it first. Privy Email sits alongside them, so a shopper who joins through a shade quiz or a sample request lands in an email program without an export step. Privy Email is listed at $30 a month with unlimited emails, and the plan is billed on mailable contacts rather than total records. Privy also lists an SMS add-on that includes 250 free SMS credits, with paid credits chosen by monthly volume.
      </p>
      <p>
        For beauty, the case for Privy is the front of the funnel. Shade quizzes, skin-type questionnaires and sample requests are high-intent moments. A tool that collects the answer and sends the first email the same day gives a beauty brand a cleaner start. A welcome series that asks what the shopper wants to fix is one of the more reliable ways to segment a beauty list from its first week.
      </p>
      <p>
        The limit is that Privy has no free plan, and its replenishment and subscription logic is less deep than Klaviyo's. A brand that already runs a strong flow platform and only wants better capture may get more value from using Privy for pop-ups and sending email from the platform it already uses. Check that list export and suppression rules work before you rely on them for consent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://privy.com/pricing">privy.com/pricing</a>. The price applies to mailable contacts, so confirm the count from your own list before you budget.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Drip: explicit control over tag-based education paths</h2>
      <p>
        Drip is the choice for a beauty operator who wants to build workflows by hand and see every branch on the canvas. Its model is tags and conditions. A customer can carry a routine-step tag, a skin-concern tag, a shade-undertone tag or a subscription-pause tag, and each tag can open a separate path. For a three-step skincare introduction, that makes it possible to route a cleanser buyer toward toner education and a serum buyer toward a moisturizer sequence without maintaining rules in a spreadsheet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing starts at $39 a month for 1 to 2,500 people with unlimited email sends, and the lower tiers list up to 50 workflows. The listed prices rise to $49 for 2,501 to 3,000 people, $89 for 4,501 to 5,000, $154 for 9,001 to 10,000 and $289 for 17,501 to 20,000. There is no ongoing free plan. The trial lasts 14 days and requires no credit card. The trial is a useful test of whether a tag-based model suits your team, because rebuilding flows later is expensive.
      </p>
      <p>
        The main limitation is that Drip rewards a team that will maintain its tags. A scheme set up in a hurry and never cleaned up creates the confusion it was meant to remove. Beauty brands also have fewer agency and template resources for Drip than for Klaviyo, so the operator should be comfortable building from scratch. Drip is a strong option for a focused catalog with clear routine logic, and a weaker one for a catalog with hundreds of shade variants that change each season.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pricing page: <a class="link" href="https://drip.com/pricing">drip.com/pricing</a>. The page lists tiers by people count, so check the exact tier for your list before you budget.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Seguno: pricing on request</h2>
      <p>
        Seguno is part of this vertical set, but no price is listed for it on this page, so every cost cell for Seguno says "pricing on request". A beauty store considering Seguno should ask the vendor for a written quote at its current contact count and a realistic monthly send volume. It should also ask whether the quote includes SMS and whether any setup fee applies. Without a price, Seguno cannot be placed in the cost table on the same footing as the other apps, and this page does not rank it against them.
      </p>
      <p>
        If Seguno is on your shortlist, run the same checks you would run on any other app. Import a clean sample of your list, build the replenishment and skip recovery flows with your own product names and sizes, and confirm the consent and suppression rules before you commit. Compare the written quote with the Klaviyo, Omnisend and Drip figures in the table above at the same contact count.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
