  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">How to choose for a beauty store</h2>
      <p>
        Start by naming the three revenue paths you depend on. For most beauty stores these are the first purchase, the first replenishment and the subscription or loyalty path. If you cannot name the product that drives the most repeat orders, solve that first, because the replenishment timer depends on it. Export the last twelve months of Shopify orders and sort them by product and size. The top three sizes by repeat purchase are the flows that matter most.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, count your profiles and split them into three groups: people who have bought, people who have only signed up, and people who have not engaged for a long time. The first group is what a profile-based app charges you to keep on the list. The second is where capture tools and education flows earn their keep. The third needs care, because heavy mailing to it raises costs and lowers deliverability. The cost table above shows how quickly the first two groups move the bill.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then decide how much of the work your team can take on. A brand with an in-house email person can manage Klaviyo or Drip and maintain tags and segments. A brand where marketing is a side task is usually better served by Omnisend, Privy or Shopify Email, where the default setup covers more of the work. Be honest about this, because a sophisticated tool set up badly will send the wrong routine to the wrong customer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, run a trial with four flows built on real data. Build a replenishment flow for one hero product, a post-purchase education flow for a new buyer, a skip recovery flow for a subscriber who has not reordered, and a suppression rule that stops promo sends to anyone who bought this week. If an app can express all four without workarounds, it is a real candidate. If it needs a chain of manual exports, it is not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Setup and migration checklist</h2>
      <p>
        Moving a beauty email program between apps is where most of the damage happens. Duplicate sends, lost consent records and a replenishment reminder that goes out on the day a subscription charges are all migration problems rather than product problems. The table below sets out a sequence that keeps the old program running while the new one is checked. Use it whether you are starting from nothing or leaving another platform.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="ledger-card mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[760px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/15 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-[#9a5b16]">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Step</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">What to do</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">When</th>
            <th class="py-3">Done when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[#4a2d17]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">1. Export consent records</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Export each profile with its opt-in source, date and email and SMS consent status</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Before any import</td>
            <td class="py-3">A file exists that lists every profile's consent source</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">2. Map products to windows</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">List each hero product, its size, typical use and a first and second reminder offset</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Week 1</td>
            <td class="py-3">Every hero product has a written replenishment window</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">3. Import engaged profiles first</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Bring in buyers and recent openers before the rest of the list</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Weeks 1 to 2</td>
            <td class="py-3">Imported counts match the export within the tolerance you set</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">4. Build suppression first</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Create rules for recent buyers of the same product, active subscribers and open skips before any promo flow</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Week 2</td>
            <td class="py-3">Test accounts receive no promo while a skip or charge is active</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">5. Test with staff accounts</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Run the welcome, cart, post-purchase and replenishment flows against internal addresses</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Weeks 2 to 3</td>
            <td class="py-3">Each flow produces the expected message at the expected offset</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">6. Pause the old flows</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Switch off the old automations one at a time and keep a list of what each one did</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Week 3</td>
            <td class="py-3">No customer receives the same reminder from both systems</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">7. Cut over in a quiet week</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Move the live program in a period with no launch, sale or large restock</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Week 4</td>
            <td class="py-3">Scheduled sends for the next launch are set up on the new platform only</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">8. Watch for 14 days</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Check bounce, complaint and unsubscribe rates by content type daily for the first week and weekly after</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Weeks 4 to 6</td>
            <td class="py-3">Rates stay within the range you measured before the move</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Building the replenishment matrix</h2>
      <p>
        A replenishment matrix is a plain table with one row per hero product. Each row holds the product name and size, the typical use pattern, the first reminder offset, the second reminder offset and the suppression rules. Writing it down keeps the reminder timing tied to how the product is used, not to a default. A 30 ml serum used morning and night runs out well before a 50 ml moisturizer used once a day, so their reminders cannot share one timer.
      </p>
      <p>
        The matrix should also say what each reminder does. A first reminder can give a usage tip for the product the customer already has, with a link to reorder. A second reminder can show a related product in the routine. Neither should carry a discount on the first touch for a loyal buyer, because a discount teaches the customer to wait for one. Store the matrix next to the flow settings so that whoever edits a flow also sees the rule behind it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Review the matrix every quarter using reorder data. Compare the day a customer reordered with the day the reminder was sent, and move the offset if reorders cluster well before or after it. Do not change the windows based on packaging alone, since the packaging does not know how your customers apply the product.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Routine education without the beginner tax</h2>
      <p>
        Education in beauty email is a sequence, not a single message, and the sequence should depend on what the customer has bought. A new buyer of a cleanser needs to know how often to use it, what to apply next and how long to wait before judging a result. A buyer on the third step of a routine needs an ingredient update or a seasonal adjustment. Sending both the same beginner lesson tells the loyal buyer that the brand has not noticed them.
      </p>
      <p>
        A practical arc for a new buyer runs in four stages. Day one brings a thank-you with clear instructions for the product bought. Day three brings a usage guide. Day seven answers the most common question about that product, such as what to do if sensitive skin reacts. Day fourteen introduces one complementary product, and only if the customer has not already bought one. Each stage depends on the product bought and on whether the customer opened the previous message.
      </p>
      <p>
        Patch-test and irritation guidance needs particular care. Advice that helps a customer with sensitive skin can alarm a customer who has never had a reaction, so the message should describe the test in neutral terms and link to the full instructions. Keep claims about results to what the product's labeling supports, and send any new claim to your compliance reviewer before it enters a flow.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Skip recovery and pausing</h2>
      <p>
        For subscription beauty brands, the most useful email is often the one that handles a skipped shipment. A customer who skips has not churned, but a generic "we miss you" message can push them toward cancellation. A better sequence starts with information: what is in the next box, why those products were chosen and how to use what the customer already has. It offers a pause before it offers a cancel. It offers an incentive only if the earlier messages got no response, and it stops once the customer reorders.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep skip recovery separate from the promo calendar. A skip sequence that runs at the same time as a sale sends two conflicting messages, and the customer will remember the sale rather than the recovery. Suppress promo flows for anyone in an active skip sequence, and tag recovered skips separately so the team can count how many came back without a discount.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pause and cancel paths should share one set of rules. If a customer pauses, the reminder and the charge should both stop until the chosen date. If they cancel, the email app should record the reason and stop all subscription-related messages. That coordination depends on the email app receiving subscription status from the billing app, so confirm the connection during the trial before you rely on it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Bundles and cross-sell that respect the routine</h2>
      <p>
        Cross-sell in beauty fails when it ignores the routine. A customer who bought a cleanser and a serum does not need a second serum, and a customer using a retinol should not receive an exfoliating acid promotion in the same week without a warning. Build cross-sell logic from a short list of entry products. For each one, write the next step in the routine and the product that comes after it. Use that map to decide which bundle a customer sees.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bundle offers work best as full-price previews for loyal customers and carefully timed offers for new ones. Frequent "complete your routine for 15% off" messages teach customers to wait for the discount, which is a margin problem as much as a retention one. Track bundle attach rate from email by entry product, and let that data decide which cross-sell gets the next slot in the calendar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Klaviyo and Drip both support product-triggered paths, and Omnisend and Privy can run cross-sell from a purchase trigger, but the logic is only as good as the routine map behind it. Write the map before you build the flows, and revise it whenever a new product changes the sequence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Deliverability and the promotion ratio</h2>
      <p>
        Beauty lists are sensitive to promotion. Subscribers who signed up for routine advice notice quickly when every message is a sale, and they unsubscribe or mark messages as spam. Track unsubscribe and complaint rates by content type every week, not only at campaign level. If promo unsubscribes run well ahead of education unsubscribes, the promo calendar is too heavy for that segment and should be trimmed before deliverability starts to slip.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separate sending domains or subdomains for education and promotion are one option when complaint rates rise. Warm any new sending domain before a sale or launch, starting with engaged profiles and raising volume over a few weeks. Brands moving from Mailchimp or Shopify Email to a new platform should plan a warm-up period before large sale sends, because a sudden jump in volume from a new sender can trip spam filters even when the content is good.
      </p>
      <p>
        The key habit for beauty stores is to keep the promo ratio visible in the weekly review, so the team sees a wrong mix before the inbox providers do. For infrastructure detail, see the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability guide</a>. For building the engaged and unengaged groups that the ratio depends on, see the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/segmentation">segmentation guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Worked example: from 2,500 to 5,000 profiles</h2>
      <p>
        The example below is illustrative. It uses only prices from the pricing pages and an invented growth path, so the arithmetic is easy to check. It is not a forecast for any real store. Suppose a beauty brand starts the year on Klaviyo's Email plan at 2,500 profiles and ends it at 5,000, because a sampling campaign and two influencer partnerships added people to the list. On the pricing page the plan moves from $60 a month to $100 a month. The difference is $40 a month, and across a full year that is 12 times $40, or $480.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now take the same 2,500-contact list on Omnisend Pro, listed at $41.30 a month. That plan includes unlimited monthly emails, so the monthly price is the same whether the store sends one campaign or ten. The Omnisend figure at 5,000 contacts is not shown on the pricing page, so the brand would request a quote before deciding anything at that size.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, take a brand that mostly mails its existing buyers and sends 30,000 emails in a month on Shopify Email. The free allowance covers the first 10,000, and the remaining 20,000 cost $1 per 1,000, which is $20 for the month. Doubling sends to 60,000 brings the charge to $50, because 50,000 billable emails at $1 per 1,000 is $50. The lesson is that the cheapest plan depends on whether the list grows or the send volume grows. Track both, and compare each against the plan's price at every step.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Common failures in beauty email programs</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Claims in urgency copy.</strong> A promo that says a product "fixes" a condition, sent under deadline pressure without compliance review, is a regulatory risk. Put a compliance checkpoint on every template change, not only at launch, and keep a record of who approved each claim.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Routine conflicts.</strong> A retinol buyer who receives an exfoliating acid promotion in the same week can have a bad reaction and a return. Keep an ingredient conflict list inside the cross-sell logic, and check each new product against it before it enters a flow.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Duplicate messages from two systems.</strong> A review request from one tool and a replenishment preview from another, arriving on the same day, annoy customers and raise unsubscribes. Keep one written trigger map that lists every automated message and the system that sends it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Profile growth without purchases.</strong> A list that grows quickly from sampling codes but rarely buys will raise the bill on any profile-based plan. Set a sunset rule that moves unengaged profiles out of the paid sending list after a fixed period without opens or purchases, and review the rule every quarter.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Migrating in peak season.</strong> A platform switch in the run-up to a major sale exposes the store to deliverability dips and missed messages. Plan the cut-over for a quiet period, and keep the old platform running read-only for a while in case a record needs checking.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">SMS for beauty replenishment</h2>
      <p>
        SMS can work for a replenishment reminder when the customer has opted in clearly and the frequency is capped. One reminder for a product that is running out is useful. A weekly text about a sale is not, and it is the fastest route to a complaint. Keep SMS for moments when a customer has said they want to hear from you that way, and keep educational content in email.
      </p>
      <p>
        Omnisend lists SMS from $0.007 per message on its Pro plan, and Privy's SMS add-on includes 250 free credits with paid credits chosen by monthly volume. Klaviyo's Email plus SMS tiers do not show a price on the pricing page, so a store that wants SMS there needs a quote. Whichever app you use, suppress SMS for any customer with an active subscription charge in the same month, and check the consent source before every send.
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep the SMS calendar in the same document as the email calendar. A beauty store that texts and emails the same customer about the same product in the same week will often find that the text is what caused the unsubscribe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Beauty brand email FAQ</h2>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">Which Shopify email app is cheapest for a beauty store with about 500 contacts?</h3>
      <p>
        If sends stay under 10,000 a month, Shopify Email costs $0 because its first 10,000 emails are free. Among profile-based plans, Omnisend's Standard tier is listed at $11.20 a month for 500 contacts and 6,000 emails, though that is a first-three-month offer rate. Klaviyo's Email plan and Mailchimp's Standard tier are each listed at $20 a month for 500 profiles or contacts. Check the renewal price before you compare.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">Does Klaviyo have a free plan?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. The free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month, and the pricing page also lists $5 of mobile messages. It is enough to build and test flows. A live store with a real list will usually need a paid Email plan, which starts at $20 a month for 500 profiles.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">How should a beauty store time replenishment emails?</h3>
      <p>
        Time them from the product and its size, not from a fixed 30-day delay. Write a replenishment matrix with one row per hero product, a first and second reminder offset, and suppression rules for recent buyers and active subscribers. Review the offsets every quarter against the day customers actually reorder.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">Can I combine email and SMS for replenishment reminders?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes, with explicit opt-in and a frequency cap. Omnisend lists SMS from $0.007 per message on its Pro plan, and Privy includes 250 free SMS credits with its SMS add-on. Klaviyo's Email plus SMS tiers are not priced on its pricing page, so ask for a quote if you plan to use SMS there. Whichever app you choose, do not text a customer who has a subscription charge processing that month.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">Is Drip a better choice than Klaviyo for tag-based routines?</h3>
      <p>
        Drip is built around tags and visible branches, so it suits a team that wants to design each path by hand. Klaviyo is stronger on product-level Shopify data and predictive segmentation. Drip's pricing starts at $39 a month for up to 2,500 people, with a 14-day trial that needs no credit card. Test both with the same four flows before you decide.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">Does a subscription app need to connect to the email app?</h3>
      <p>
        It should. The billing app handles charges, but the email app has to know when a subscription is active, paused or cancelled so it can suppress conflicting messages. Confirm that the connection passes subscription status in both directions during the trial, and test a skip, a pause and a cancellation before you go live.
      </p>
      <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f] mt-8">What if an app does not show a price for my list size?</h3>
      <p>
        Ask for a written quote at your current contact count and a realistic monthly send volume, and ask whether SMS is included. Seguno does not have a listed price on this page, so its quote is the only figure to compare. Omnisend and Klaviyo's SMS tiers also need a quote at larger sizes. Do not budget from an estimate until you have the quote in writing.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
